FINDINGS:
1. [MAJOR] src/testing/mod.rs:119 — the new symlink-root guard calls `symlink_metadata(dir)` on the caller-supplied path verbatim, but `run_all_tests()` at src/testing/mod.rs:53 passes the literal string `"tests/"` with a trailing separator. On Linux a trailing slash forces path resolution through the final symlink, so `lstat`/`symlink_metadata("tests/")` reports the *target* directory, not the link — the check at line 120 (`metadata.file_type().is_symlink()`) never fires and `read_dir` at line 139 then follows the link. The comment and REPORT claim "The caller-supplied root is refused when it is itself a link", but the one production caller that hands in a directory path always supplies it with a trailing slash, and `edge_discovery_refuses_a_symlinked_root` (tests/discovery_test.rs:1415) only exercises `find_test_files(root)` without a trailing slash, so the guarded path is untested. Fix: normalise trailing separators before inspecting (e.g. trim `/` from the `Path`/use its `components`, or check `dir` and `dir` with separators stripped), and add a test calling `find_test_files("tests/")` against a symlink. I traced the path but could not run it (read-only session); please confirm with that test — the coverage gap is certain even if the lstat behaviour differs.
2. [MINOR] src/lib.rs:160 — `reject_zero_test_run` now fires on `passed + failed == 0`, but reports `No tests were defined in '<dir>'`. That message is inaccurate for the case the round-5 change exists to catch (a suite whose only markers are skips: `total == 1, skipped == 1`), where tests *were* defined and *were* skipped, not "not defined". A reader gets the wrong remediation. Fix: distinguish the two states, e.g. when `results.total > 0 && skipped == total` say `No tests were run (N skipped)`; keep "defined" only for the truly empty case.
3. [MINOR] src/testing/harness.rs:194 — `run_benchmark` discards the outcome (`let _ = self.execute_test_code(&bench_code);`), so a benchmark body that fails to lex/parse/run is silently treated as successful and nothing is added to `passed`/`failed`. With the new zero-execution guard this now surfaces as `rb test bench.rb` exiting non-zero with `No tests were defined`, masking the real syntax/runtime error in the benchmark instead of reporting it. Not introduced by this phase, but the guard makes the masking reachable and there is no test for a benchmark-only file. Fix: record the benchmark failure (or at least propagate the error) rather than dropping it, and name it in the zero-run message.

REVIEW VERDICT: FINDINGS 3
